# Never emit compiled `.js` into `src/`

This is a **Vite + TypeScript** project. `tsconfig.app.json` / `tsconfig.node.json`
set `"noEmit": true` on purpose. Vite's default module resolution tries `.js`
**before** `.tsx`, so any compiled `*.js` sitting next to a `.tsx` is loaded
**instead of** the source — silently hiding every edit you make. A full UI
redesign once appeared to do nothing because **608 stale `.js` shadowed the
real `.tsx`**. Do not recreate this situation.

## Forbidden

- ❌ `tsc <file>.tsx`, `tsc src/...`, or bare `tsc` pointed at files.
  Passing files to `tsc` makes it **ignore tsconfig and default to emit ON**,
  writing a `.js` next to every source file.
- ❌ `tsc --noEmit false` or any flag/config that enables emit.

## Use instead

- **Type-check** (all honor `noEmit`):
  - ✅ `npm run build`  → `tsc -b && vite build`
  - ✅ `npx tsc -b`
  - ✅ `npx tsc -p tsconfig.app.json --noEmit`
- **Verify a change renders**:
  - ✅ `npm run dev`  or  `npx vite build`

## Invariant

There must be **zero `*.js` under `src/` that have a `.ts`/`.tsx` sibling.**
If you see any, an emitting compile was run — they are not source. `predev`/
`prebuild` auto-delete them (`scripts/cleanEmittedJs.mjs`) and `src/**/*.js`
is gitignored, but the right fix is to stop running emitting `tsc` at all.
